{
  "file": "crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs",
  "item": "fn[whole_comment]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This callback fills the Profile interface for every non-Rust profile.\n Those profiles all set hashes_comment to never, so Builder::encode skips\n comment_prose for every source input; plain text does not use the grammar\n encoder at all. Rust hashes doc comments through its separate doc_prose.\n The fallback therefore has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/outline/language.rs#fn[whole_comment]` is valid. It sits on an explicit function with a body, in fenced commentary. The commentary gives a substantive reason, and the source agrees with it:\n\n- **Profiles that use the fallback:** `whole_comment` is assigned to `comment_prose` in the css, markdown, python, toml, typescript and yaml `PROFILE` items, and in the plain-text `PROFILE` in `plain.rs`. The plain-text profile reaches it through `super::profile::whole_comment`, which re-exports the same function. Every one of these profiles sets `hashes_comment: never`.\n- **Where `comment_prose` is called:** the only call is in `Builder::encode` in `outline/mod.rs`. It runs only when `(self.profile.hashes_comment)(...)` returns true, so a `never` profile never reaches `whole_comment`.\n- **Rust:** its profile uses `hashes_comment: is_doc_comment` and `comment_prose: doc_prose`, so it does not use the fallback.\n- **Plain text:** its profile has `language: None`. `comment_ranges` returns early without a grammar, and the grammar encoder is not used.\n- **Test profiles:** the profiles built in `markdown_tests.rs` and `tests.rs` inherit the markdown or Rust profile with `..`, so they add no reachable use.\n\nThe commentary says the fallback has no reachable behavior in the supported profiles. That is accurate, and the wording is limited to supported profiles. It does not claim anything about custom profiles that someone might write. The citation role, a `cite` of a built-in, is permitted. There is no redundant `impl` or `test` reference on the item.",
